1\. IP spoofing

An attacker falsifies the source IP address of network packets to make them appear to come from another device.

Testing tools: Wireshark, tcpdump, Scapy.

Safe test: Capture traffic in your own lab and inspect source IP addresses against the actual device generating the traffic.

![Les attaques d'usurpation d'identité](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/8KGQsc12nRhqKVkYskfr3zfvd8DUL8lsfwAND9P3XqgO0gCY8F2XBDZlQGPD_dgRO6rP03bKn00czX5WT9ESGu10TIcWMn4KpoteQlAJKX1ASztfOibdZl_kUU8iNsbg8YqWut8lop4oNWlWZ7a_ep4kYa2oH6ETzheqtlLLe5yHBI13TCkrrhQbt7UWjO7b?purpose=fullsize)

2\. ARP spoofing (ARP poisoning)

An attacker sends false ARP messages to associate their MAC address with another device's IP address.

Testing tools: Wireshark, arp, arp-scan.

Safe test: Inspect your own LAN's ARP table and look for unexpected changes or duplicate IP-to-MAC mappings.

![What is DNS Spoofing and Cache Poisoning❓](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/YW8mrD0yogkMrF7rYSG8eimZLVy0Hx6xsChZ_F8B2okgbFle6X8odsl9w9Mlf9H0EgbTvUpERk6c0MJyN262A0rCA9-YGJtCTiM_nz-5soSNQus1rsnyfnWgLGt64fa78pfZDmtAKJ-vjn5pEaNjkva5zN3YsjjSv-1Tk3zcl4q-lx0-LxK3jmK5TFTJ4kmo?purpose=fullsize)

3\. DNS spoofing

False DNS responses direct users to an incorrect IP address instead of the legitimate website.

Testing tools: nslookup, dig, Wireshark.

Safe test: Compare DNS responses from your configured resolver with a trusted independent resolver.

![MAC Address Spoofing: How It Works and Why It Matters](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/MhYiuoPxAdNt8OGfydHjrMGKxmMRNVJMJQNwa95Bx1ADkvIOBghXTzI3LXWuVUkDcsIyvP5WoAxMQ_jSGysCzZtjdcGgR0nnYcxJzjZOAh-kR5g7RVTkS7WxE5R-bmplVHJHuyLNYxxty3gUfF7PGBxBtKhWev_7kYNG7fXeiscrXulMoyR--sGeQVLzF76p?purpose=fullsize)

4\. MAC spoofing

A device changes its apparent MAC address to imitate another network device.

Testing tools: ip link, ipconfig, Wireshark.

Safe test: Compare the MAC address shown by your operating system with the MAC address recorded by your router.

![DHCP Spoofing: What It Is and How to Defend Against It | Adnan Khan posted on the topic | LinkedIn](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/vVVqoSLaup_9_njho44qZcHC8ZtmNhFyCxdXyh21ZEpFU5l99QxUk3vggSg0_tkffbXnXNF5TaI8S2hxcZEsF_czGeWeQ6sqhZmHLMkrm7TMU0TYw_zcOWuWtq8c8IfoJ1CuzZUaHD9mTih6OibE1iTvC4mPFAOJOnEP0LnfHQDT0IvXhsrs5XMUckUWSDm0?purpose=fullsize)

5\. DHCP spoofing

A rogue DHCP server provides false network configuration, such as an incorrect gateway or DNS server.

Testing tools: Wireshark, tcpdump.

Safe test: Capture DHCP traffic and verify that only your authorized DHCP server is responding.

![What is Email Spoofing? The Complete Guide | CloudSEK](https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/o4V8Q2mW5yb8JuKO9OvaKYXabvHPfh1Mg67OB0iswO0PhAxMpxh1FbaKAnyF8LGCr4LC4dMYiPgM8Hos9tkgvSsbqNlIWK_pi1hwrzah8i0yMtio-fOtikLYea_lskclMeybnu5BfGG7c3bZCE8dBFiEF1EiWLpD_a7qwuHcistqDWG2MTFanJvjCJxOa8FX?purpose=fullsize)

6\. Email spoofing

An attacker forges an email sender address to make a message appear to come from a trusted person or organization.

Testing tools: Email header analyzer, MXToolbox.

Safe test: Inspect your own test email headers and check SPF, DKIM and DMARC authentication results.

