### 2\. Practical commands for testing spoofing on Ubuntu or Kali Linux

Since you have been working with Ubuntu, Termux, Wireshark, tcpdump and Kismet, here are useful commands for examining your own network.

### Test 1: MAC address inspection

```bash
ip link show
```

Displays your network interfaces and their MAC addresses.

### Test 2: ARP inspection

```bash
ip neigh show
```

Displays IP-to-MAC address mappings.

Look for unexpected changes or conflicting mappings.

### Test 3: DNS inspection

```bash
nslookup example.com
```

Or:

```bash
dig example.com
```

Compare the responses with your trusted DNS resolver.

### Test 4: Network packet inspection

```bash
sudo tcpdump -i any -n
```

Captures packets passing through your interfaces.

To capture DNS traffic only:

```bash
sudo tcpdump -i any port 53
```

### Test 5: Wi-Fi inspection

```bash
nmcli device wifi list
```

Displays nearby wireless networks.

Look for duplicate SSIDs and unexpected access points. A duplicate name alone does not prove an Evil Twin attack.

### Test 6: HTTP header inspection

```bash
curl -I https://example.com
```

Displays HTTP response headers.

For an authorized test server, you can inspect how it handles different User-Agent values:

```bash
curl -A "TestBrowser" https://your-test-server.example
```

### Test 7: Email authentication

Check the headers of an email you received and look for:

* SPF: Sender Policy Framework.

* DKIM: DomainKeys Identified Mail.

* DMARC: Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance.

These help identify forged email senders. Cisco +1

